    "keyNotes": "• Comparisons:\n  - Simile: Direct comparison using 'like' or 'as' (e.g. 'He fought like a wounded lion').\n  - Metaphor: Implicit comparison stating one thing is another without using 'like' or 'as' (e.g. 'The classroom was a bustling beehive').\n• Attribution & Sound Devices:\n  - Personification: Giving human attributes or emotions to inanimate objects or abstract ideas (e.g. 'The wind whispered through the trees').\n  - Onomatopoeia: Words that imitate natural sounds (e.g. 'buzz', 'hiss', 'clatter').\n  - Alliteration: Repetition of initial consonant sounds in neighboring words.\n• Irony, Paradox & Contrast:\n  - Hyperbole: Deliberate and gross exaggeration for emphasis or humor.\n  - Irony: Contrast between expectation and reality (Verbal, Situational, Dramatic).\n  - Oxymoron: Two contradictory terms placed side by side (e.g. 'bittersweet', 'deafening silence').\n  - Paradox: A seemingly absurd or self-contradictory statement that reveals a deeper truth upon reflection (e.g. 'The child is father of the man').",
