    "keyNotes": "• Roots in Oral Aesthetics:\n  - African written poetry derives rhythm, structure, imagery, and devices from oral traditions: praise songs (oriki, apae), funeral dirges, hunting chants, and incantations.\n  - Frequent use of apostrophe, parallelism, repetition, and refrain to evoke drumbeats and call-and-response participation.\n• The Negritude Movement:\n  - Founded by Léopold Sédar Senghor, Aimé Césaire, and Léon Damas in the 1930s.\n  - Celebrated African beauty, blackness, ancestral spiritual connectedness, and refuted colonial theories of African cultural inferiority.\n• Protest & Post-Independence Critique:\n  - Resistance against apartheid in South Africa (e.g. Dennis Brutus, Oswald Mtshali).\n  - Condemnation of tyrannical African military dictators, nepotism, brain drain, and environmental degradation in modern Africa.",
