    "keyNotes": "• Elizabethan Theatrical Conventions:\n  - The Globe Theatre: Open-air polygonal playhouse with a thrust stage, no artificial lighting, minimal scenery, and groundlings standing in the pit.\n  - All roles (including female roles like Lady Macbeth, Desdemona, Viola) were played by young adolescent boys due to social laws.\n• Key Shakespearean Dramatic Techniques:\n  - Soliloquy: A character speaks their innermost thoughts, motives, and psychological turmoil aloud while alone on stage (e.g. Macbeth's \"Tomorrow, and tomorrow, and tomorrow\").\n  - Aside: A brief confidential remark spoken directly to the audience or another character that other actors on stage pretend not to hear.\n  - Dramatic Foil: A character whose qualities contrast sharply with another character to emphasize specific traits (e.g. Banquo's moral integrity foils Macbeth's treacherous ambition).\n  - Blank Verse: Unrhymed iambic pentameter (ten syllables per line with alternating unstressed and stressed syllables), reserved for noble characters, while prose is used for comic lower-class clowns.\n• Major Thematic Motifs:\n  - Blind ambition and moral corruption (*Macbeth*).\n  - Revenge, madness, and indecision (*Hamlet*).\n  - Jealousy and deception (*Othello*).",
