    "isFreeTrial": false,
    "isVip": true,
    "keyNotes": "• The Metaphysical Poets (17th Century):\n  - Key Poets: John Donne (\"Death, be not proud\", \"A Valediction: Forbidding Mourning\"), George Herbert (\"The Pulley\"), Andrew Marvell.\n  - Characteristics:\n    1. Metaphysical Conceit: An ingenious, highly intellectual, and extended comparison between two strikingly dissimilar objects (e.g. comparing two separated lovers' souls to the twin legs of a mathematical compass).\n    2. Paradox: A statement that appears self-contradictory on the surface but reveals a profound underlying truth (\"Death, thou shalt die\").\n    3. Wit and Argumentative Structure: Poems built like rigorous legal or philosophical debates with dramatic colloquial opening lines.\n• Modern War Poetry (World War I):\n  - Key Figures: Wilfred Owen (*Dulce et Decorum Est*), Siegfried Sassoon, Rupert Brooke.\n  - Stripping the Romantic Myth of War: Rejects the patriotic lie that dying in battle is glorious and noble (*Dulce et decorum est pro patria mori*).\n  - Graphic Realism: Uses harrowing, visceral imagery of gas attacks, muddy trenches, coughing soldiers, and mutilated corpses to awaken civilian consciences.",
