    "keyNotes": "• Meaning & Scope: History is the systematic study and documented record of human past activities, events, achievements, and challenges over time.\n• Major Sources of Ghanaian History:\n  - Oral Tradition: Spoken information transmitted from one generation to another through folklore, proverbs, praise poetry, dirges, and royal palace chronicles. Advantages: Accessible, captures local emotions; Disadvantages: Subject to memory distortion, exaggeration, and lack of precise chronological dates.\n  - Archaeology: Scientific excavation and analysis of material remains (pottery, stone tools, cowries, bronze artifacts). Provides concrete chronological dating through Carbon-14 techniques.\n  - Written Records: Arabic manuscripts (trans-Saharan travelers like Ibn Battuta), European logs and letters (Portuguese, Dutch, British), and local newspapers.\n  - Linguistics: Comparative study of languages to trace migration routes and ethnic affinities.\n• Value of History: Builds national identity, promotes cultural preservation, and prevents repetition of past societal mistakes.",
