    "keyNotes": "• Background to the 1948 Crisis:\n  - Post-WWII Disillusionment: Thousands of Gold Coast soldiers who fought for the British in Burma and East Africa returned home to mass unemployment, unpaid war pensions, and high inflation.\n  - Economic Grievances: European and Syrian import merchants (Association of West African Merchants - AWAM) monopolized essential imported goods, price-gouging local consumers. Nii Kwabena Bonne III led an effective nationwide boycott of European goods in early 1948.\n• The Tragic Crossroads Shooting (28 February 1948):\n  - Ex-servicemen staged a peaceful, unarmed protest march toward Christianborg Castle (seat of British Governor Sir Gerald Creasy) to present a petition.\n  - At the Christianborg Crossroads, British Superintendent Colin Imray ordered fire on the peaceful veterans: Sergeant Adjetey, Corporal Attipoe, and Private Odartey Lamptey were shot dead on the spot.\n• National Outrage & Rioting:\n  - Sparked five days of violent rioting, arson, and looting of AWAM trading stores in Accra, Kumasi, Koforidua, and Nsawam.\n  - British colonial administration arrested the leaders of the United Gold Coast Convention (UGCC) - \"The Big Six\" (Kwame Nkrumah, J.B. Danquah, Edward Akufo-Addo, Emmanuel Obetsebi-Lamptey, William Ofori-Atta, and Ako Adjei).\n• The Watson Commission & Coussey Committee:\n  - The British constituted the Aiken Watson Commission of Enquiry to investigate the disturbances.\n  - Findings: Declared the 1946 Burns Constitution \"outmoded at birth\" and recommended immediate constitutional reforms toward internal self-government. Led directly to the all-African Coussey Constitutional Committee of 1949.",
