    "keyNotes": "• Branches of Geography: Physical Geography (landforms, atmosphere, soils, waters) and Human/Economic Geography (population, settlement, trade, agriculture).\n• Map Essentials & Scales:\n  - Map scale: The ratio between the distance on a map and the corresponding actual distance on the ground.\n  - Three Types of Scale: Statement scale (e.g. 1 cm represents 0.5 km), Representative Fraction / Ratio (e.g. 1:50,000), Linear/Bar scale.\n• Grid References:\n  - Eastings (vertical grid lines numbered west to east) and Northings (horizontal grid lines numbered south to north).\n  - Rule: Always read Eastings FIRST, then Northings (\"walk into the house before climbing the stairs\").\n• Relief & Contours:\n  - Contour lines: Imaginary lines joining points of equal elevation above mean sea level.\n  - Close contours indicate steep slopes; widely spaced contours indicate gentle slopes.\n  - Landform signatures: V-shaped pointing uphill = Valley/River gorge; V-shaped pointing downhill = Spur; concentric circular contours = Isolated hill or knoll.",
