    "keyNotes": "• Plate Tectonics Theory:\n  - The lithosphere (crust and upper mantle) is broken into major and minor tectonic plates floating on the semi-molten asthenosphere.\n  - Plate Boundaries:\n    1. Divergent (Constructive): Plates move apart; magma rises to form mid-ocean ridges and rift valleys (e.g. East African Rift Valley).\n    2. Convergent (Destructive): Plates collide; denser oceanic plate subducts under continental plate forming ocean trenches, fold mountains (e.g. Himalayas), and volcanic arcs.\n    3. Transform (Conservative): Plates slide horizontally past one another, triggering severe earthquakes (e.g. San Andreas Fault).\n• Vulcanicity:\n  - Intrusive Landforms (magma cools below earth's surface): Batholiths (huge plutonic granite mass), Sills (horizontal sheets concordant to bedding planes), Dykes (vertical/steep sheets discordant to rock strata), Laccoliths (dome-shaped intrusions).\n  - Extrusive Landforms (magma erupts onto surface as lava): Composite cones (stratovolcanoes like Mt. Cameroon), Shield volcanoes, Calderas, Geysers and Hot springs.\n• Earthquakes & Seismic Activity:\n  - Origin: Sudden release of stress along geological faults. Focus (hypocentre) is the subterranean point of origin; Epicentre is the point directly above on the Earth's surface.\n  - Measurement: Richter Scale (magnitude of energy released) and Mercalli Scale (observed physical damage).\n  - Seismic Zones in Ghana: The Coastal Boundary Fault running through Weija, McCarthy Hill, Nyanyano, and the Akwapim-Togo ranges experiences frequent seismic tremors.",
