    "keyNotes": "• Marine Processes of Coastal Erosion:\n  - Hydraulic Action: The physical force of pounding ocean waves crashing against rock faces, compressing air in cracks until rocks shatter.\n  - Abrasion / Corrasion: Boulders, pebbles, and sand hurled by breaking waves against coastal cliffs, acting like sandpaper.\n  - Attrition: Rock fragments in wave water collide against one another, becoming smaller, rounded, and smoother.\n  - Solution / Corrosion: Soluble minerals (e.g. calcium carbonate in limestone) dissolved by the chemical action of acidic seawater.\n• Features of Marine Erosion:\n  - Sea Cliffs & Wave-Cut Platforms: Notch cut at cliff base retreats inland, leaving a flat bedrock terrace exposed at low tide.\n  - Headlands and Bays: Formed on discordant coastlines where soft rock erodes quickly into bays while resistant hard rock stands out as headlands.\n  - Cave-Arch-Stack-Stump Sequence: Wave refraction attacks lines of weakness in a headland -> Cave cut through to form an Arch -> Roof of arch collapses leaving an isolated pillar called a Stack -> Wave erosion at base fells stack into a Stump.\n• Features of Marine Deposition:\n  - Beaches, Spits, Sandbars, Tombolos (spit linking mainland to an island), and Lagoons (open and closed lagoons).\n• Coastal Management in Ghana:\n  - Rapid coastal erosion threatens communities along the Keta, Ada, and Axim coastlines. Government interventions include the Keta Sea Defence Wall Project and rip-rap boulders to protect against rising tidal waves.",
