    "keyNotes": "• Rural Settlement Patterns:\n  - Nucleated (Clustered): Dwellings clustered tightly around a central water source, market, or traditional palace (e.g. traditional Akan towns). Promotes social cohesion and defense.\n  - Dispersed (Scattered): Isolated homesteads separated by vast farmland or pasture (e.g. northern Ghana farming communities).\n  - Linear: Dwellings arranged in a long row along a highway, river bank, or railway line (e.g. fishing villages along the coastal road).\n• Settlement Hierarchy & Central Place Theory (Walter Christaller):\n  - Threshold Population: The minimum population required to sustain a particular business or service (e.g. corner grocery store requires a low threshold; specialized heart hospital requires a high threshold).\n  - Range of a Good: The maximum distance a consumer is willing to travel to obtain a good or service.\n• Urbanization in West Africa:\n  - Rapid expansion of metropolitan centers like Greater Accra, Kumasi, Lagos, and Abidjan.\n  - Push Factors: Rural poverty, erratic rainfall, lack of electricity, inadequate schools and healthcare facilities.\n  - Pull Factors: Employment opportunities, bright city lights, modern hospitals, universities, and commercial variety.\n• Urban Challenges in Ghana:\n  - Proliferation of informal settlements and slums (e.g. Old Fadama / Agbogbloshie in Accra).\n  - Severe traffic congestion, perennial flooding due to paved surfaces and clogged storm drains, and solid waste disposal crises.",
