    "keyNotes": "• Definition: Lionel Robbins defined economics as \"the science which studies human behavior as a relationship between ends and scarce means which have alternative uses.\"\n• The Core Economic Problem:\n  - Scarcity: Human wants are unlimited, but the productive resources (land, labor, capital, entrepreneurship) to satisfy them are finite and scarce.\n  - Choice: Because resources are scarce, economic agents must make choices among competing alternatives.\n  - Scale of Preference: A ranked list of an individual's or government's unsatisfied wants in descending order of priority, with the most urgent item placed at the top.\n  - Opportunity Cost: The real cost of satisfying a want, expressed in terms of the next best alternative foregone (sacrificed).\n• Production Possibility Curve (PPC / PPF):\n  - A graphical locus showing the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce given fixed resources and technology.\n  - Points inside the PPC indicate underutilization / unemployment of resources. Points on the curve show productive efficiency. Points outside are currently unattainable.",
