    "keyNotes": "• The Law of Demand: States that, all other things being equal (ceteris paribus), as the price of a good rises, the quantity demanded falls; and as price falls, quantity demanded rises. Inverse relationship!\n• Determinants of Demand (Non-price factors):\n  - Consumer disposable income (Normal goods: demand increases as income rises; Inferior goods: demand falls as income rises).\n  - Prices of related goods: Substitutes (e.g. Milo vs. Cowbell; tea vs. coffee) and Complements (e.g. cars and petrol; mobile phones and SIM cards).\n  - Tastes, preferences, and fashion.\n  - Size and age structure of the population.\n  - Expectation of future price changes.\n• Movement Along vs. Shift of the Curve:\n  - Movement along the curve: Caused ONLY by a change in the price of the good itself (contraction or extension of demand).\n  - Shift of the curve: Caused by a change in any non-price factor (increase in demand shifts right; decrease shifts left).\n• Exceptional / Abnormal Demand Curves: Giffen goods, Veblen/Ostentatious goods (snob appeal), and goods of speculative expectation.",
