    "keyNotes": "• The Law of Supply: States that, ceteris paribus, as the price of a commodity increases, the quantity supplied increases; and as price falls, quantity supplied decreases. Direct relationship!\n• Determinants of Supply: Cost of production (wages, raw materials), technological advancement, government policies (taxes increase cost; subsidies lower cost), weather and natural conditions (critical for Ghanaian agriculture), number of producers in the industry.\n• Market Equilibrium:\n  - Occurs at the price where Quantity Demanded (Qd) equals Quantity Supplied (Qs).\n  - Equilibrium Price: The market clearing price where neither shortage nor surplus exists.\n  - Excess Demand (Shortage): Occurs when price is below equilibrium; pushes prices upward.\n  - Excess Supply (Surplus): Occurs when price is above equilibrium; forces sellers to discount prices.\n• Price Controls:\n  - Maximum Price Legislation (Price Ceiling): Set below equilibrium to protect consumers; causes shortages and hoarding.\n  - Minimum Price Legislation (Price Floor): Set above equilibrium to protect producers (e.g. cocoa farmers, minimum wage); causes surpluses.",
