    "keyNotes": "• Key National Income Aggregates:\n  - Gross Domestic Product (GDP): Total monetary value of all final goods and services produced within the geographical boundaries of a country during a specified year.\n  - Gross National Product (GNP) = GDP + Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA).\n  - Net National Product (NNP) = GNP - Depreciation (Capital Consumption Allowance).\n• Three Methods of Measurement:\n  1. Output / Value Added Method: Sum of value added by all productive sectors (avoiding double counting by deducting intermediate inputs).\n  2. Income Method: Sum of all factor incomes earned by residents (Wages + Rent + Interest + Profit). Excludes transfer payments!\n  3. Expenditure Method: GDP = C + I + G + (X - M) [Household Consumption + Private Investment + Government Spending + Net Exports].\n• Measurement Problems in Ghana:\n  - Massive informal sector transactions lacking accounting records.\n  - Non-monetized subsistence agriculture and unpaid household labor.\n  - Widespread tax evasion and smuggling along porous borders.\n  - Poor statistical data collection infrastructure.",
