    "keyNotes": "• Inflation Defined: A persistent and appreciable rise in the general price level of goods and services over time, resulting in a fall in the purchasing power of money.\n• Types & Causes of Inflation:\n  - Demand-Pull Inflation: Occurs when Aggregate Demand exceeds Aggregate Supply at full employment (\"too much money chasing too few goods\").\n  - Cost-Push Inflation: Caused by rising production costs (wage increases, electricity tariff hikes, fuel price increases) pushing up prices.\n  - Imported Inflation: Caused by depreciation of the local currency (Ghana Cedi) against major trading currencies, raising the domestic cost of imported goods (crude oil, machinery, rice).\n• Effects of Inflation:\n  - Losers: Fixed-income earners (pensioners, salaried civil servants), lenders/creditors (paid back in devalued currency), cash savers.\n  - Gainers: Debtors/borrowers, real estate owners, equity investors.\n• Measures to Control Inflation:\n  - Monetary Policy: Raising the Monetary Policy Rate (MPR), selling Treasury Bills (OMO), increasing reserve requirements.\n  - Fiscal Policy: Cutting government expenditure, increasing direct taxes to reduce disposable income.\n  - Supply-Side: Subsidizing agricultural inputs to boost local food production.",
