    "keyNotes": "• Free Trade vs. Protectionism:\n  - Free Trade: Unrestricted international exchange of goods and services without government tariffs or quotas. Promotes specialization, consumer variety, and efficiency.\n  - Protectionism: Government policy of erecting trade barriers to shield domestic industries from foreign competition.\n• Instruments of Protectionism:\n  - Tariffs (Customs Duties): Taxes levied on imported goods (Ad-valorem: percentage of value; Specific: fixed amount per physical unit).\n  - Import Quotas: Physical limits on the quantity of a specific commodity that may be imported during a given period.\n  - Subsidies: Financial assistance granted to domestic producers to lower costs and compete with cheap imports.\n  - Embargo: Total legal ban on trade with a specific country or commodity.\n  - Non-tariff barriers: Strict health, safety, and sanitary standards (e.g. FDA regulations).\n• Arguments for Protectionism:\n  - Infant Industry Argument: Protecting young domestic manufacturing industries until they achieve economies of scale.\n  - Preventing Dumping (selling below cost to wipe out local competition).\n  - Correcting Balance of Payments deficits and protecting domestic jobs.",
