    "isFreeTrial": false,
    "isVip": true,
    "keyNotes": "• Franchise / Suffrage: The legal right to vote in political elections. Universal Adult Suffrage: Every citizen who reaches the legal voting age (18 years in Ghana) has the right to vote regardless of gender, race, or income.\n• Electoral Systems:\n  - First-Past-The-Post / Plurality System: The candidate who receives the highest number of votes in a single-member constituency wins, even if without absolute majority (used for Ghana's parliamentary elections). Simple and produces decisive outcomes, but can produce disproportional representation.\n  - Absolute Majority System: Winning candidate must secure more than 50% of total valid votes cast (50% + 1) (used for Ghana's Presidential election; triggers a runoff if not met).\n  - Proportional Representation: Seats in the legislature are allocated to parties in direct proportion to the percentage of total votes they win nationwide.\n• Electoral Commission of Ghana:\n  - Independent body established under Article 43 of the 1992 Constitution. Functions: Compiling and revising voters' register, demarcating constituency boundaries, conducting public elections and referenda, and declaring results.",
