    "keyNotes": "• The Civil Service Defined: The administrative machinery of the executive arm of government comprising permanent, non-political officials who formulate and implement state policies under ministerial supervision.\n• Core Principles of the Civil Service:\n  1. Political Neutrality / Impartiality: Civil servants must faithfully serve the government of the day regardless of political party without openly engaging in partisan politics.\n  2. Permanence of Tenure: Continuity in office; civil servants do not vacate their jobs when a political regime changes.\n  3. Anonymity: Civil servants operate behind the scenes; the political Minister takes all public praise or parliamentary blame for departmental actions.\n  4. Meritocracy: Recruitment and promotions overseen by the independent Public Services Commission based on competitive examinations and qualifications.\n• Public Corporations (SOEs):\n  - State-owned entities created by statute to undertake commercial or utility operations (ECG, GWCL, VRA).\n  - Problems: Red tape, bureaucratic delays, political interference, embezzlement, and overstaffing.",
