    "keyNotes": "• Nature & Characteristics of Public Corporations:\n  - Definition: Autonomous business enterprises established by an Act of Parliament, owned and financed by the state to provide essential utilities, social services, or industrial production.\n  - Characteristics: Separate legal entity (can sue and be sued in its own name), government-funded capital, governed by a Board of Directors appointed by the President, non-profit or quasi-commercial orientation.\n  - Key Ghanaian Corporations: Electricity Company of Ghana (ECG), Ghana Water Company Limited (GWCL), Volta River Authority (VRA), Ghana Post.\n• Rationale for Establishing Public Corporations:\n  - Capital Intensity: Enormous capital required to build national power dams and water networks that private investors cannot afford.\n  - Preventing Private Monopolies: Preventing private firms from exploiting citizens with exorbitant prices on essential survival services.\n  - Balanced Regional Development and Job Creation.\n• Major Problems Facing Ghanaian Public Corporations:\n  - Political Interference: Frequent dismissal of board members and appointment of unqualified party loyalists.\n  - Inefficiency and Bureaucratic Red Tape: Civil service mentality leading to lethargy, low customer service, and lack of innovation.\n  - Financial Deficits and Huge Debts: Inability to collect tariffs from government agencies and illegal consumption connections.\n• Reform Policies:\n  - Commercialization: Forcing state corporations to operate on commercial, profit-driven principles without government operational bailouts.\n  - Privatization / Divestiture: Selling state enterprises to private investors through the Divestiture Implementation Committee (DIC) to inject private capital, technology, and management efficiency.",
